WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.275 --> 00:00:01.710
RETO MEIER: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:05.310
has hosted a rich array of talks
detailing the future of Cloud.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:08.580
If you didn't get a chance to
catch Instant Insights, a GCP

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:11.040
developer's guide to building
real-time data analysis

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:14.100
pipelines, then stay tuned,
because here's the recap.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.096
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:21.650
Google Cloud Platform
offers a variety of services

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.680
that you can use to analyze
high-volume continuous data

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:25.400
streams.

00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:26.941
COLT MCANLIS: Fully
managed services,

00:00:26.941 --> 00:00:28.980
such as Pub/Sub,
Dataflow, and BigQuery,

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:32.060
allow you to focus on generating
the insights you need,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.090
rather than the distraction of
managing distributed systems.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:38.270
RETO MEIER: For example, this
is a real-time visualization

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:40.400
of taxi rides in New York City.

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.110
The incoming data
stream is managed

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:44.360
by Pub/Sub, Google's
global publish

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.940
and subscribe service with
guaranteed at least once

00:00:46.940 --> 00:00:48.360
message delivery.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:51.180
This is a lot of data--

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.050
way too much to be processed and
visualized by our client device

00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:54.740
directly.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.230
To enable that, we need
to adjust the data stream

00:00:57.230 --> 00:00:59.377
in a way that's more
manageable to the client.

00:00:59.377 --> 00:01:01.460
COLT MCANLIS: That's where
Cloud Dataflow fits in.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:04.340
Cloud Dataflow is a high-level
service and programming model

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:06.080
for pipeline data processing.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.660
In our example, we need to
reduce the incoming stream

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:10.820
volume so the visualization
can display it

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:13.340
in real time, which
we can do by creating

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:15.560
a pipeline in Dataflow.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:18.380
We start with an unbounded
collection of raw taxi data

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:22.010
from Pub/Sub, and then apply
a sequence of transforms.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:24.020
First, we window the
data by time stamp,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.670
and then we bucket by proximity.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:27.770
This way, for each
window of time,

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.500
we can find taxis that
are near to each other.

00:01:30.500 --> 00:01:33.140
Next, for each window
and location combination,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.000
we count the number
of taxis and output

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:37.179
that as a new
collection via Pub/Sub.

00:01:37.179 --> 00:01:39.470
RETO MEIER: For our needs,
we wrote a custom transform.

00:01:39.470 --> 00:01:42.650
However, that's not always
the case, as Dataflow supplies

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.590
a number of standard transforms,
like windowing, mapping,

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:46.760
and count.

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:50.120
Both the incoming and outgoing
collections are unbounded,

00:01:50.120 --> 00:01:52.010
so the pipeline will
run until the heat

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:54.530
death of the solar system,
until it's stopped,

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.810
or until someone spills
coffee on the entire internet,

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:58.250
all at once.

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:00.620
And because it is fully
managed, the back end

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:03.230
takes care of everything,
from optimizing the execution

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:06.530
graphs, spinning up VMs, staging
the pipeline code to the VMs,

00:02:06.530 --> 00:02:09.567
managing I/O with Pub/Sub,
and managing persistent state

00:02:09.567 --> 00:02:12.080
for full tolerance,
so you don't need

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.330
to think about any of that.

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.520
Instead, we can go straight
to the UI that shows us

00:02:16.520 --> 00:02:18.440
how the pipeline is working.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:20.150
Here, the volume
of our taxi stream

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:23.640
is reduced from roughly
20,000 MPS to roughly 2,000

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:27.237
MPS, which is low enough for
our visualizer to handle.

00:02:27.237 --> 00:02:29.570
COLT MCANLIS: While Dataflow
can help process your data,

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.460
in order to gather
insights, you can

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:33.560
leverage Big Query,
Google's solution

00:02:33.560 --> 00:02:35.660
for enterprise data warehousing.

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.990
Using Dataflow, we can create
one or more ETL pipelines

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:41.030
that ingest the
content into BigQuery,

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.560
allowing us to perform
queries and analysis.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.470
For example, in addition
to the raw data,

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.050
we could also identify
rides to the airport,

00:02:48.050 --> 00:02:51.260
and then run simple SQL queries
to compare the ride volume

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.710
and average fare between airport
taxis and the rest of New York,

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:56.360
not to mention the
time-based trends,

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.180
letting us know the best time
to catch a cab to the airport.

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.040
RETO MEIER: And this
is just a few examples

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:04.250
to inspire ideas about how you
can use these tools to gain

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:06.202
insights into your own data.

00:03:06.202 --> 00:03:07.910
Check out the full
